I was actually helping a friend's kid with some basic algebra.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:07,060
Oh, nice.

4
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:08,120
Fun times?

5
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:08,800
Yeah, right.

6
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,440
But I started staring really, really closely at the parenthesis,

7
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:14,340
just like the symbol itself.

8
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:17,940
And usually when we write out a mathematical equation,

9
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,920
we take for granted the tools we use to organize it, you know?

10
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,360
We completely take them.

11
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:23,680
Right.

12
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,460
You draw a little curved line on the left,

13
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:27,000
another little curved line on the right,

14
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,420
and you just sort of assume you've captured something real.

15
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:30,860
Like you've wrangled the numbers.

16
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:31,440
Exactly.

17
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:35,180
It's like you've built a little fence to keep the numbers from wandering off.

18
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:37,960
It feels so structured, so clean.

19
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,440
Well, I mean, it's a comforting illusion, right?

20
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:48,220
We are trained from childhood to view those little curved lines as absolute containers.

21
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:48,960
Yeah.

22
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,980
It establishes this rigid binary.

23
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:57,240
We were taught that something is either inside the parentheses or, you know, it's outside.

24
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:58,560
There's no middle ground.

25
00:00:58,720 --> 00:00:59,240
Exactly.

26
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:00,980
No ambiguity at all.

27
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:01,520
Right.

28
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:05,380
But then I encountered the source material for today's deep dive.

29
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:10,780
And man, my entire relationship with that simple curved line was just shattered.

30
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:12,560
It really pulls the rug out from under you.

31
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:13,200
It does.

32
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:17,960
Because suddenly that parenthesis didn't look like a helpful organizational tool anymore.

33
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:20,680
It looked like a massive compromise.

34
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:22,620
Like a failure of imagination almost.

35
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:23,120
Yes.

36
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:29,240
Because according to the manuscript we are exploring today, the parenthesis is actually a lie.

37
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:30,680
A flat out lie.

38
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:35,960
A fundamental lie about space, geometry, and literally how the universe operates.

39
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,340
It is such a profound assertion.

40
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:44,580
The author is suggesting that standard mathematical notation isn't just like an arbitrary choice of symbols.

41
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:44,940
Right.

42
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:48,560
It's a historical accident, a glitch in our cognitive development.

43
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:58,120
By forcing three-dimensional spatial reality onto a flat, one-dimensional string of text, we have fundamentally limited how human beings process reality.

44
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:03,400
And to give you, the listener, some context here, the manuscript is titled Semantic Infrastructure.

45
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:09,560
The subtitle is Constraint, Projection, and Accessibility Across Computation, Cognition, and Cosmology.

46
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:10,600
It is quite a title.

47
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:11,640
It is dense.

48
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:17,280
It was published in May 2026 by an independent researcher known only as Flixian.

49
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:17,960
Just Flixian.

50
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:18,480
Yeah.

51
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:21,280
And the scale of this text is staggering.

52
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:29,420
We were talking about a paradigm-shifting framework that spans philosophy, computer science, pure geometry.

53
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:32,020
And the actual physical laws of the universe.

54
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:32,360
Right.

55
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:42,260
Flixian argues that physical gestures, human language, digital computation, and physics are all just different coordinate systems for one underlying reality.

56
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:46,900
Which is exactly why the mission for our conversation today is so incredibly ambitious.

57
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:47,900
We're going deep.

58
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:48,560
We really are.

59
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:54,200
We are essentially going to guide you, the listener, through a complete rewiring of how you perceive existence.

60
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:54,820
No pressure.

61
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:55,640
Right.

62
00:02:56,460 --> 00:03:03,040
But to understand Flixian's work, we have to start by entirely dismantling the idea that the universe is made of, well, things.

63
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:04,020
Which is hard to do.

64
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:04,600
Very hard.

65
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:08,980
We have to throw away objects, particles, and distinct entities as our primary building blocks.

66
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,220
We have to rebuild reality using the geometry of access.

67
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:13,400
The power of boundaries.

68
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:13,860
Yes.

69
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:17,560
And eventually, this revolutionary visual language called sphere pop.

70
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:24,580
I am so excited to get to sphere pop, but I want to start exactly where you just said, with that dismantling process.

71
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:25,720
The accessibility inversion.

72
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:25,980
Right.

73
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:28,000
The text opens with the accessibility inversion.

74
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:30,420
And it asks a seemingly simple question.

75
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:33,260
Why do we assume things are real?

76
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:33,740
Right.

77
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:38,820
Flixian claims that physics and computer science both have it totally backward.

78
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:43,820
They treat objects as the most primitive, basic building blocks of reality.

79
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:45,480
Like, in physics, it's particles.

80
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:45,940
Yeah.

81
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:49,300
Or in computer science, it's data structures or specific states.

82
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:53,240
But Flixian says, no, objects are an illusion.

83
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:53,660
Yeah.

84
00:03:53,720 --> 00:04:00,620
And classical ontology, you know, the traditional way we study the nature of being, it always asks the question, what things are there?

85
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:00,940
Right.

86
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:06,460
If you look out at a landscape, classical ontology asks you to list the trees, the rocks, the clouds.

87
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:07,960
Just inventorying stuff.

88
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:08,540
Exactly.

89
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:12,280
But Flixian introduces what they call an accessibility ontology.

90
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,620
And this perspective doesn't care about the trees or the rocks.

91
00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:16,520
So what does it care about?

92
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,860
It asks, what transitions are available to a system from here?

93
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:20,340
Okay.

94
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:21,540
I'm trying to visualize this.

95
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:27,900
So instead of looking at a chess board and saying, there are knights, pawns, and rooks.

96
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:28,240
Right.

97
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:29,520
Identifying the pieces.

98
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,020
Yeah.

99
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:37,240
An accessibility ontology looks at the board and only sees, like, a web of possible future moves.

100
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:38,560
That is a perfect analogy.

101
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:38,780
Yeah.

102
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:39,140
Yes.

103
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:41,960
The reality of the knight isn't the carved piece of wood.

104
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:46,500
The reality of the knight is the L-shaped trajectory it is permitted to take.

105
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:46,960
Wow.

106
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:47,460
Okay.

107
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:50,720
This fundamentally changes how we model existence.

108
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:57,580
Classical state-based systems, like Newtonian physics, they are entirely deterministic.

109
00:04:57,640 --> 00:04:59,100
Because they just look at the current state.

110
00:04:59,280 --> 00:04:59,480
Right.

111
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:03,920
They assume that if you know the exact state of an object right now, you can determine its

112
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:07,440
state at the next moment via some mathematical transition function.

113
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:08,980
But there's a problem with that, right?

114
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:10,000
A massive problem.

115
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:13,760
Flixian points out this gaping blind spot in that classical view.

116
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:16,560
Where does that transition function actually come from?

117
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:17,480
Who made the rule?

118
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:18,060
Exactly.

119
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,860
Who or what enforces it?

120
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:24,340
In Flixian's framework, objects don't actually exist as independent entities.

121
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:25,060
They're just illusions.

122
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:28,860
They are stable projections of underlying geometric constraints.

123
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:34,740
Basically, they are basins or grooves in a landscape of accessibility.

124
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:38,160
So reality isn't made of static things.

125
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:40,180
It's made of reachable paths.

126
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:40,600
Yes.

127
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:42,040
Reachable paths.

128
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:46,840
I really want to linger on this, because it's hard to let go of the idea that my coffee

129
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:48,900
cup sitting here is a thing.

130
00:05:49,020 --> 00:05:50,440
Oh, it's incredibly counterintuitive.

131
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:57,280
But Flixian backs this up by going to a very famous concept in philosophy, the quote

132
00:05:57,280 --> 00:05:59,560
by Alfred Korzybski from 1931.

133
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:01,020
The map is not the territory.

134
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:01,580
Exactly.

135
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:03,400
The map is not the territory.

136
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:04,680
We've all heard that phrase.

137
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:06,180
It's almost a cliche at this point.

138
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:06,520
Right.

139
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,320
It just means the model of reality is not reality itself.

140
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:09,700
Yeah.

141
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:11,780
But Flixian doesn't just quote it.

142
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:16,540
The text formalizes it mathematically into something called the map territory projection

143
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:16,980
theorem.

144
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:17,580
Yes.

145
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:20,520
And the math here is deeply, deeply revealing.

146
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:26,500
Flixian uses topology to prove that any faithful map making procedure is a continuous

147
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:30,440
projection that, well, it must contain what they call non-trivial fibers.

148
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:31,640
Okay, non-trivial fibers.

149
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:33,740
That sounds like incredibly dense jargon.

150
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:34,100
It does.

151
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:38,740
So let's carefully break down what a non-trivial fiber is, because reading this, it seemed

152
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:42,320
like the absolute key to understanding why objects are an illusion.

153
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:43,480
It is the key.

154
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:48,900
Because when I first read non-trivial fibers, my brain immediately pictured like some kind

155
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,440
of cosmic oatmeal.

156
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:51,040
Huh.

157
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:52,120
Cosmic oatmeal.

158
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:53,600
No, it's purely geometric.

159
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:55,980
Okay, what does it actually mean then?

160
00:06:56,140 --> 00:06:57,580
Think about the act of projection.

161
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:04,080
In mathematics, when you project a higher-dimensional complex space onto a lower-dimensional simpler

162
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:07,740
space, you inevitably have to crush information down.

163
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:08,340
Right.

164
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:09,580
You're losing dimensions.

165
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:10,200
Exactly.

166
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:17,240
Imagine shining a flashlight on a complex three-dimensional wire sculpture to cast a two-dimensional shadow

167
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:17,760
