00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:20,060
What does it actually mean?

261
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:21,400
Let's take it piece by piece.

262
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:24,120
A plenum is an old philosophical term.

263
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:27,880
It means a space that is entirely filled with matter or energy.

264
00:11:28,140 --> 00:11:30,140
It's the exact opposite of a vacuum.

265
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,580
So no empty space.

266
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:31,960
Right.

267
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:37,120
Affliction views the universe not as empty space with things floating in it, but as a dense,

268
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:38,380
tension-filled fabric.

269
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:39,500
Okay, that's the plan.

270
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:42,620
In this RSVP model, you don't track objects.

271
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:43,940
You track accessibility.

272
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:49,780
First, you use a scalar field to encode the potential accessibility of a region.

273
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:51,600
What does a scalar field look like?

274
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:56,500
Think of it basically as a heat map of how easy or hard it is to move into that area.

275
00:11:56,580 --> 00:11:59,920
Okay, like a topographical map showing steep hills and deep valleys.

276
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:00,440
Exactly.

277
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:07,680
Then you use a vector field to encode the directed flow, the actual paths or currents moving toward

278
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:08,820
those accessible states.

279
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:10,340
And then entropy comes in, right?

280
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:10,800
Yes.

281
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:14,160
Finally, you use entropy to measure the remaining freedom of the system.

282
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:16,320
How many options does it have left?

283
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:22,400
So instead of defining reality by locating little particles on an XY grid like standard physics,

284
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:27,280
You define reality by mapping out the invisible walls and currents of where a system can and

285
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:27,960
cannot go.

286
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:33,280
But if the universe is just a web of accessible paths, why can't I just take any path I want?

287
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:35,940
What's stopping the water from flowing absolutely everywhere?

288
00:12:36,180 --> 00:12:37,460
Well, what stops a river?

289
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:38,440
The riverbanks.

290
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,080
You can't have a specific trajectory without something defining where you can't go.

291
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:48,300
A river only flows in the specific direction because the riverbanks constrain the water.

292
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:54,800
And that profound realization is the pivot point of Fliction's entire manuscript.

293
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:55,800
The constraint.

294
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:59,700
It is the concept, the constraint must precede content.

295
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,820
This might be the most philosophically wild argument in the whole text for me.

296
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:04,520
It's huge.

297
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:09,680
The idea that the rule is more fundamental than the thing it actually governs.

298
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:14,660
It completely challenges everything we intuitively feel about how the world works.

299
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:18,520
Because we usually think that things exist first, and then rules are applied to them afterward.

300
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:18,800
Right.

301
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:21,020
But Fliction argues the exact opposite.

302
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:22,580
Let's look at a computer program.

303
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:22,980
Okay.

304
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:28,200
Before a program even runs, before it computes a single value or processes a single piece

305
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:29,040
of real data.

306
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:30,640
Its behavior is already defined.

307
00:13:30,740 --> 00:13:33,920
Its behavior is already entirely constrained by its type signature.

308
00:13:34,300 --> 00:13:39,360
The rule is dictating whether it accepts integers, text strings, or booleans.

309
00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:42,440
The constraint exists before the content.

310
00:13:42,440 --> 00:13:43,320
Yes.

311
00:13:43,720 --> 00:13:45,840
The text mentions physics field equations, too.

312
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:46,300
Right.

313
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:47,580
The laws of physics.

314
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:50,540
The equations governing electromagnetism or gravity.

315
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:56,920
They constrain what physical trajectories are even accessible in the universe, long before

316
00:13:56,920 --> 00:14:00,700
any specific planet or star even forms to follow them.

317
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:04,520
So the rule exists before the star.

318
00:14:05,060 --> 00:14:06,600
That is wild.

319
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:08,180
And it applies to human language, too.

320
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:11,740
Grammar constrains a sentence before a single word is spoken.

321
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:15,620
Let me stop you there, because I want to make sure I really grasp this language analogy.

322
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:16,000
Sure.

323
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:21,620
Are you saying that if I start a sentence with the dog, the grammar of English has already

324
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:24,280
built an invisible wall around the rest of my sentence?

325
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:24,880
Exactly.

326
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:30,300
The moment you say the dog, English syntax immediately dictates that the next word likely

327
00:14:30,300 --> 00:14:31,640
needs to be a verb or an adverb.

328
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:31,900
Right.

329
00:14:31,980 --> 00:14:33,180
It can't be the dog the.

330
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:33,580
No.

331
00:14:33,660 --> 00:14:35,660
It cannot be another article like the.

332
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:39,420
The grammar constrains the accessible future of the sentence.

333
00:14:39,420 --> 00:14:41,540
Before I even know what I'm going to say about the dog.

334
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:41,980
Exactly.

335
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:46,180
The content, whether the dog runs, barks, or sleeps, hasn't been realized yet.

336
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:51,500
But the boundary of what is permitted has already shaped the trajectory of your thought.

337
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:58,260
What's incredible is how the manuscript traces this concept that the constraint is the true

338
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:00,940
reality back through centuries of human thought.

339
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:02,600
It's not a new idea, really.

340
00:15:02,740 --> 00:15:02,980
No.

341
00:15:03,180 --> 00:15:05,360
This isn't just some modern computer science theory.

342
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:11,240
Affliction of Reaches all the way back to the 11th century to the Islamic philosopher Ibn Sina.

343
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:12,680
Also known as Avicenna.

344
00:15:12,760 --> 00:15:13,000
Right.

345
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:15,340
It is a stunning historical parallel.

346
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:20,060
Ibn Sina made a very careful defining distinction between two concepts.

347
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:20,740
What were they?

348
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:27,800
Mahiya, which translates to quiddity, or essence, and wujud, which translates to existence.

349
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:28,680
Quiddity.

350
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:30,880
I love that word, the whatness of a thing.

351
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:31,160
Right.

352
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:35,980
Ibn Sina argued that the essence of a thing, its logical constraints, its core definition,

353
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:40,240
is completely distinct from whether or not it actually physically exists in the real world.

354
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:45,940
So you can perfectly define the constraints of a unicorn, a horse with a single horn, without

355
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:48,220
a unicorn ever taking physical form.

356
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:48,780
Exactly.

357
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:54,380
And later in the 13th century, the Latin philosopher Thomas Aquinas took this Islamic philosophy

358
00:15:54,380 --> 00:15:58,360
and transformed it into the distinction between act and potency.

359
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:00,460
Potency being the realm of possibility.

360
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:01,220
Yes.

361
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:03,860
Potency is constrained possibility.

362
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:07,040
Act is the realization of that possibility.

363
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:13,100
So fliction is taking this ancient medieval philosophical intuition about essence preceding

364
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:18,740
existence and updating it into a rigorous, computable, mathematical framework.

365
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:20,580
That's exactly what's happening.

366
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:24,960
And the way fliction mathematically defines constraint is just so elegant.

367
00:16:25,560 --> 00:16:29,600
The text defines constraint addition simply as set intersection.

368
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:30,080
Yes.

369
00:16:30,080 --> 00:16:36,280
When you add a constraint to a system, you are mathematically intersecting sets, which inevitably

370
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:38,700
shrinks the size of the accessible reality.

371
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:41,760
Let me try a real world analogy here to see if I'm tracking the math.

372
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:42,100
Of course.

373
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:45,540
Let's say I'm in a new city and I'm looking for a restaurant for dinner.

374
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:46,000
Okay.

375
00:16:46,180 --> 00:16:49,180
At first, the accessible reality of my dinner options is huge.

376
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:50,160
It's the entire city.

377
00:16:50,400 --> 00:16:53,700
My first constraint is, the restaurant must be open right now.

378
00:16:53,740 --> 00:16:54,020
Boom.

379
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:54,560
Boom.

380
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:54,600
Boom.

381
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,580
I've just taken the set of all restaurants and intersected it with the set of currently

382
00:16:58,580 --> 00:16:59,420
open businesses.

383
00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:02,080
My accessible reality just got much smaller.

384
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:02,400
Right.

385
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:03,820
Then I add a second constraint.

386
00:17:04,420 --> 00:17:05,960
It must serve Italian food.

387
00:17:06,760 --> 00:17:08,480
I've intersected another set.

388
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:10,260
The reality shrinks again.

389
00:17:10,420 --> 00:17:11,660
You're narrowing the possibilities.

390
00:17:11,800 --> 00:17:11,960
Right.

391
00:17:12,240 --> 00:17:12,840
I add a third.

392
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:15,340
It must be within one mile of my hotel.

393
00:17:15,340 --> 00:17:20,960
The more constraints I add, the smaller, more specific, and more dense my accessible

394
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:22,040
reality becomes.

395
00:17:22,440 --> 00:17:25,300
That is a perfect, practical translation of the math.

396
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:30,880
And according to the RSVP framework, the scalar field hits its absolute minimum, its

397
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:35,640
most defined point at the dense core of all these intersecting constraints.

398
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:36,080
Exactly.

399
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:42,960
And your analogy highlights precisely why classical state-based models fail so spectacularly when

400
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:43,700
they hit boundaries.

401
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:44,560
Why is that?

402
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:48,820
Because classical models secretly smuggle in assumptions about what is accessible.

403
00:17:49,100 --> 00:17:50,820
They assume you already know the city map.

404
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:51,120
Yes.

405
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:54,940
They assume the map of the city and the rules of the roads are just magically, eternally

406
00:17:54,940 --> 00:17:55,240
given.

407
00:17:55,760 --> 00:18:00,260
But what happens when a system hits a boundary where those underlying assumptions suddenly

408
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:00,660
break?

409
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:04,400
Like what kind of boundary breaks classical physics or cognition?

410
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:10,400
Well, in human cognition, think of a massive aha moment, a massive paradigm shift.

411
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:14,460
Oh, like when you realize you've been looking at a problem entirely the wrong

412
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:14,780
way.

413
00:18:14,840 --> 00:18:15,080
Right.

414
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:20,180
A moment where your entire perspective suddenly changes and the rules you thought governed

