00:20:05,200 --> 00:20:10,280
Now imagine a single solid black wall suddenly appears in that void.

454
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:11,160
A constraint.

455
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:11,700
Yes.

456
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:16,700
The moment you introduce that constraint, that wall, you have instantly created structure.

457
00:20:16,860 --> 00:20:18,580
Because now I have a relationship to the wall.

458
00:20:18,980 --> 00:20:19,380
Exactly.

459
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:21,620
You now have a this side and a that side.

460
00:20:21,960 --> 00:20:23,540
You have a distance to the wall.

461
00:20:24,140 --> 00:20:30,780
The rat's entire meaningful behavior in a maze, the left turns, the right turns, the learning,

462
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:34,180
the memory, it is entirely generated by the walls of the maze.

463
00:20:34,180 --> 00:20:36,400
The constraint forces the trajectory.

464
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,340
And the trajectory is the reality.

465
00:20:38,500 --> 00:20:44,360
Without the constraints of the maze, the rat just wanders randomly on an open plane, generating

466
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:46,300
no meaningful pattern, no information.

467
00:20:46,300 --> 00:20:48,600
So the limitation is the engine of creation.

468
00:20:49,280 --> 00:20:49,540
Yes.

469
00:20:49,860 --> 00:20:50,100
Wow.

470
00:20:50,220 --> 00:20:50,440
Okay.

471
00:20:50,440 --> 00:20:55,380
So if constraint is the engine of creation, we have to look at the absolute most primitive

472
00:20:55,380 --> 00:20:58,700
basic type of constraint a human mind can process.

473
00:20:58,700 --> 00:21:00,980
The very first wall in the void.

474
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:01,740
Containment.

475
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:02,100
Right.

476
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:06,960
Before humanity had algebra, before we had written words, before we even had the concept

477
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:11,620
of the number one, we had the simple spatial geometry of inside versus outside.

478
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:16,600
Flexion argues that containment is the foundational cognitive operation of the human brain.

479
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:17,560
It starts so early.

480
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:18,420
It does.

481
00:21:18,580 --> 00:21:22,640
If you look at child development, infants do not learn mathematics by staring at equations

482
00:21:22,640 --> 00:21:23,360
on a chalkboard.

483
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:23,860
Thank goodness.

484
00:21:23,860 --> 00:21:28,520
Long before they can count, they are physically interacting with the spatial world.

485
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:31,360
They take a wooden block and they put it inside a toy box.

486
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:32,140
They pull it out.

487
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:33,080
They put it back in.

488
00:21:33,220 --> 00:21:33,380
Right.

489
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:39,460
And through this purely physical spatial action, they are hardwiring their brains to understand

490
00:21:39,460 --> 00:21:42,500
that the block is inside and the carpet is outside.

491
00:21:42,780 --> 00:21:47,380
This spatial concept of a boundary of containment, it precedes addition.

492
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:48,800
It precedes logic.

493
00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:51,920
It precedes everything we formally call math.

494
00:21:51,920 --> 00:21:58,140
And fliction lays out three fundamental mathematical rules of containment that arise directly from

495
00:21:58,140 --> 00:21:59,420
this primitive childhood action.

496
00:21:59,500 --> 00:21:59,640
Okay.

497
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:00,260
What are they?

498
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:01,760
Rule one is transitivity.

499
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:07,600
If I put block A inside box B and then I put box B inside trunk C, then block A is

500
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:08,620
now inside trunk C.

501
00:22:08,700 --> 00:22:11,640
That feels super obvious, but it's a rigorous geometric law.

502
00:22:11,760 --> 00:22:12,100
Correct.

503
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:14,100
Rule two is antisymmetry.

504
00:22:14,820 --> 00:22:19,120
Entity A and entity B cannot be inside each other unless they are the exact same thing.

505
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:19,420
Right.

506
00:22:19,420 --> 00:22:24,040
You cannot put the toy box inside the block while the block is simultaneously inside the

507
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:24,380
toy box.

508
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:26,000
It's physically and logically impossible.

509
00:22:26,460 --> 00:22:32,260
And rule three is non-triviality, which simply means that true containment actually

510
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:33,360
exists in the universe.

511
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:37,000
There are things genuinely inside other things.

512
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:38,640
We aren't just imagining it.

513
00:22:38,820 --> 00:22:39,260
Exactly.

