00:34:56,020 --> 00:34:56,220
Yeah.

778
00:34:56,460 --> 00:34:59,780
The moment they finish speaking, your mind collapses the architecture of the sentence

779
00:34:59,780 --> 00:35:02,420
into a single, resonant meaning or understanding.

780
00:35:02,420 --> 00:35:06,560
So reality is basically playing a giant cosmic game of Tetris.

781
00:35:06,600 --> 00:35:07,020
Oh, that is.

782
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:07,320
Yeah.

783
00:35:07,320 --> 00:35:11,700
The blocks are falling, piling up in these complex, nested, highly constrained shapes,

784
00:35:11,820 --> 00:35:14,360
building up tension, and then boom, the line clears.

785
00:35:14,560 --> 00:35:16,840
They collapse into a flat, simple line of meaning.

786
00:35:16,980 --> 00:35:18,160
That's a beautiful analogy.

787
00:35:18,360 --> 00:35:19,560
But I have to push back here.

788
00:35:19,660 --> 00:35:23,860
We spent the first 20 minutes of this conversation talking about the map territory projection theorem.

789
00:35:24,080 --> 00:35:24,340
Right.

790
00:35:24,520 --> 00:35:28,720
We established that projecting high dimensional space into low dimensional space creates

791
00:35:28,720 --> 00:35:30,020
non-trivial fibers.

792
00:35:30,480 --> 00:35:31,460
Information is lost.

793
00:35:31,460 --> 00:35:32,220
Yes, it is.

794
00:35:32,420 --> 00:35:38,040
When we collapse all that beautiful, nested, 3D complexity of math or perception into a single

795
00:35:38,040 --> 00:35:41,400
flat number or word, aren't we destroying the map?

796
00:35:41,660 --> 00:35:44,520
Aren't we losing the trajectory we just said was so important?

797
00:35:44,760 --> 00:35:46,760
We absolutely are losing information.

798
00:35:47,180 --> 00:35:49,160
That is the price of a usable reality.

799
00:35:49,400 --> 00:35:50,600
The price of reality.

800
00:35:50,840 --> 00:35:55,580
Fliction mathematically proves that a collapse map always reduces depth.

801
00:35:55,860 --> 00:35:59,200
Because depth is bounded below by zero, you can't have negative containment.

802
00:35:59,200 --> 00:36:04,040
This continuous reduction is exactly why computation eventually terminates.

803
00:36:04,500 --> 00:36:08,460
It's why your perception eventually makes sense of the visual world instead of being paralyzed

804
00:36:08,460 --> 00:36:09,140
by data.

805
00:36:09,420 --> 00:36:09,820
Exactly.

806
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:13,420
Fliction calls these collapse maps admissibility preserving.

807
00:36:13,940 --> 00:36:15,100
What does that mean, practically?

808
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:19,160
It means the collapse always safely lands you within the original constraint boundaries.

809
00:36:19,160 --> 00:36:23,300
It gives you a stable, usable reality that obeys the rules of the system.

810
00:36:23,360 --> 00:36:23,980
The art's safe.

811
00:36:24,080 --> 00:36:24,320
Yes.

812
00:36:24,980 --> 00:36:26,900
But your intuition is entirely correct.

813
00:36:27,500 --> 00:36:32,260
The visual geometric truth of the computation, the actual journey the system took to reach

814
00:36:32,260 --> 00:36:35,240
the answer, is brutally destroyed in the process.

815
00:36:35,580 --> 00:36:42,080
We are left holding these flat, linear symbols entirely disconnected from the spatial reality

816
00:36:42,080 --> 00:36:43,060
of how they were created.

817
00:36:43,060 --> 00:36:50,340
And that profound sense of loss, the destruction of the geometric truth, is what leads fliction

818
00:36:50,340 --> 00:36:54,560
to introduce the absolute centerpiece of this entire manuscript.

819
00:36:54,840 --> 00:36:59,580
To stop squashing the spatial geometry of thought into flat little symbols on a page.

820
00:36:59,700 --> 00:37:02,680
Fliction invents a radical new notation system.

821
00:37:03,420 --> 00:37:04,860
Computation is physical space.

822
00:37:05,340 --> 00:37:06,420
It's called sphere pop.

823
00:37:06,660 --> 00:37:07,320
Sphere pop.

824
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:12,120
This is where affliction attempts to cure the historical glitch of standard notation.

825
00:37:12,420 --> 00:37:16,000
Think about what your brain has to do when you read standard mathematical notation.

826
00:37:16,640 --> 00:37:19,980
Like a basic algebra equation written left to right on a piece of paper.

827
00:37:20,160 --> 00:37:20,440
Okay.

828
00:37:20,660 --> 00:37:25,020
It requires three very heavy unnatural cognitive lifts.

829
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:26,280
Unnatural is the right word.

830
00:37:26,380 --> 00:37:27,440
What are those three lifts?

831
00:37:27,680 --> 00:37:32,520
First, you have to look at a linear string of text and mentally parse it into a hierarchical

832
00:37:32,520 --> 00:37:32,840
tree.

833
00:37:32,840 --> 00:37:36,720
You have to visually scan it and say, okay, this piece is connected to that piece.

834
00:37:36,940 --> 00:37:37,000
Right.

835
00:37:37,320 --> 00:37:43,140
Second, you have to recall invisible memorized precedence rules like PEMDIS, the order of

836
00:37:43,140 --> 00:37:45,120
operations that aren't actually drawn on the paper.

837
00:37:45,380 --> 00:37:46,880
They're just rules you have to remember.

838
00:37:47,180 --> 00:37:47,540
Exactly.

839
00:37:47,900 --> 00:37:53,200
And third, you have to mentally compute the evaluation order before you can even begin to

840
00:37:53,200 --> 00:37:53,900
do the arithmetic.

841
00:37:54,320 --> 00:37:55,400
It is exhausting.

842
00:37:55,540 --> 00:37:57,880
No wonder so many kids are traumatized by math.

843
00:37:58,000 --> 00:38:00,100
It really is an unfair cognitive load.

844
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:05,420
We force them to do complex, invisible data parsing in their working memory before they

845
00:38:05,420 --> 00:38:07,700
are even allowed to touch the actual numbers.

846
00:38:07,700 --> 00:38:12,180
We are making them translate geometry into a foreign text language.

847
00:38:12,360 --> 00:38:18,400
Historically, Flixia notes that computer science got very close to solving this in 1958 with

848
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:20,880
the invention of the programming language LISP.

849
00:38:21,120 --> 00:38:21,800
LISP.

850
00:38:22,100 --> 00:38:23,220
Famous for its parentheses.

851
00:38:23,500 --> 00:38:23,640
Right.

852
00:38:23,640 --> 00:38:29,000
LISP uses heavy parentheses that act like visual hugs, strictly enclosing operations.

853
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:33,300
So the textual tree structure matches the evaluation structure exactly.

854
00:38:33,520 --> 00:38:38,240
But LISP still ultimately relies on reading linear text left to right.

855
00:38:38,420 --> 00:38:38,640
Right.

856
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:44,960
Spherpop takes LISP's underlying structural insight and translates it into actual two-dimensional

857
00:38:44,960 --> 00:38:45,860
planar geometry.

858
00:38:46,220 --> 00:38:46,840
Nested bubbles.

859
00:38:46,980 --> 00:38:48,200
That is the core of Spherpop.

860
00:38:48,360 --> 00:38:50,660
It literally looks like bubbles inside of bubbles.

861
00:38:50,760 --> 00:38:51,260
It's beautiful.

862
00:38:51,420 --> 00:38:54,980
No parentheses are needed because the spatial containment itself is the grouping.

863
00:38:55,820 --> 00:38:59,900
A Spherpop expression is a 2D diagram of closed circular regions.

864
00:39:00,280 --> 00:39:05,260
Each bubble either contains a number or it contains an operator and smaller bubbles nested

865
00:39:05,260 --> 00:39:05,700
inside it.

866
00:39:05,700 --> 00:39:10,080
Let's walk through the visual example provided in the text to show how elegant this is.

867
00:39:10,800 --> 00:39:12,400
Remember our earlier equation.

868
00:39:13,320 --> 00:39:15,400
1 plus 3 by 22.

869
00:39:15,620 --> 00:39:18,000
In standard notation, that's a flat line of symbols.

870
00:39:18,280 --> 00:39:21,040
In Spherpop, you start by drawing a large outer bubble.

871
00:39:21,620 --> 00:39:27,160
Inside that outer bubble floating in the space is a 1, a plus sign, and a medium-sized bubble.

872
00:39:27,280 --> 00:39:27,720
Okay, I see.

873
00:39:27,860 --> 00:39:29,360
Now look inside the medium bubble.

874
00:39:29,940 --> 00:39:34,280
Inside it, there is a 3 foot, a multiplication sign, and a small inner bubble.

875
00:39:34,640 --> 00:39:34,940
Following.

876
00:39:34,940 --> 00:39:36,800
Finally, look inside the smallest bubble.

877
00:39:36,940 --> 00:39:39,800
It contains a 2 foot, an exponent sign, and another 2.

878
00:39:40,040 --> 00:39:43,440
The genius of this is that the geometry tells you exactly what to do.

879
00:39:43,920 --> 00:39:45,440
You don't need to memorize PEMDAS.

880
00:39:45,760 --> 00:39:49,640
You don't need to know what an exponent is versus an addition sign in terms of priority.

881
00:39:49,900 --> 00:39:51,020
You just look at the drawing.

882
00:39:51,480 --> 00:39:54,320
You naturally see the smallest, innermost bubble.

883
00:39:54,320 --> 00:39:57,080
It is the deepest point of the containment hierarchy.

884
00:39:57,360 --> 00:39:58,740
It has a depth of 2.

885
00:39:58,740 --> 00:40:03,240
It contains 2 squared, so you do the operation and you literally pop the bubble.

886
00:40:03,240 --> 00:40:09,260
The boundary collapses and leaves behind the single number 4, now floating inside the medium bubble.

887
00:40:09,300 --> 00:40:09,720
Exactly.

888
00:40:10,060 --> 00:40:11,380
Now you look at the diagram again.

889
00:40:11,600 --> 00:40:13,440
The innermost bubble is now the medium one.

890
00:40:13,740 --> 00:40:15,040
It contains 3 times 4.

891
00:40:15,300 --> 00:40:15,900
You pop it.

892
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:19,680
It collapses, leaving the number 12 floating in the large outer bubble.

893
00:40:19,680 --> 00:40:22,340
Finally, you are left with just the outer boundary.

894
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:23,740
It contains 1 plus 12.

895
00:40:24,020 --> 00:40:25,820
You pop it and you get 13.

896
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:27,160
It's so visceral.

897
00:40:27,540 --> 00:40:34,820
What stands out to me and what I want you, the listener, to picture is how this makes math incredibly tactile and visual.

898
00:40:35,500 --> 00:40:40,940
It's not memorizing arbitrary, invisible rules written by someone hundreds of years ago.

899
00:40:40,940 --> 00:40:43,620
It's literally popping bubble wrap from the inside out.

900
00:40:43,820 --> 00:40:46,140
When I read this section, it honestly made me sad.

901
00:40:46,240 --> 00:40:57,420
It made me wonder how much human genius we have lost over the centuries because we forced deeply visual, spatial thinkers to use linear, flat symbols.

902
00:40:57,580 --> 00:41:02,000
How many kids thought they were bad at math when really they were just bad at parsing linear text?

903
00:41:02,080 --> 00:41:02,520
Exactly.

904
00:41:02,680 --> 00:41:05,620
It's a profound, heartbreaking educational critique.

905
00:41:05,980 --> 00:41:10,300
But Flixion pushes it so much further than just a tool for a middle school classroom.

906
00:41:10,300 --> 00:41:13,420
This brings us to the culmination of the manuscript.

907
00:41:13,900 --> 00:41:15,920
The plenum interpretation of evaluation.

